1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:06,111
1933, the depression, a nation desperate for work.

2
00:00:06,112 --> 00:00:11,000
The answer, build a dam, a gigantic dam.

3
00:00:11,001 --> 00:00:22,000
On a scale that amazes even today, now a saga of power, the Grand Coulee Dam on modern marvels.

4
00:00:58,780 --> 00:00:59,980
The Land

5
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:01,460
Do some work for me.

6
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:05,420
Roll Columbia, won't you roll, roll, roll.

7
00:01:05,860 --> 00:01:09,060
Roll Columbia, won't you roll, roll, roll.

8
00:01:09,800 --> 00:01:15,260
In 1941, folk singer Woody Guthrie sang the praises of the Grand Coulee Dam.

9
00:01:15,261 --> 00:01:20,940
His simple lyrics were the first attempt to set that mighty structure in a human perspective.

10
00:01:20,941 --> 00:01:29,940
That's a challenge because there's nothing about the dam, the Columbia River, or the gorge of Cooley that is not of epic proportions.

11
00:01:29,941 --> 00:01:38,940
A half century after its construction, the Grand Cooley Dam is still one of the largest concrete structures on earth.

12
00:01:38,941 --> 00:01:42,940
It seems almost too huge to have been made by man.

13
00:01:42,941 --> 00:01:47,940
But beneath its awesome exterior lies a beguiling simplicity.

14
00:01:47,941 --> 00:01:54,040
The Grand Coulee Dam is a working modern marvel that lives up to its promise.

15
00:01:54,041 --> 00:02:00,640
It is a monument to the power of nature, the will of a nation, and the rugged men who built it.

16
00:02:00,641 --> 00:02:14,641
The Columbia River begins as melting snow

17
00:02:20,240 --> 00:02:23,380
from desolate mountain peaks in the Pacific Northwest.

18
00:02:24,480 --> 00:02:27,600
The gentle waters meander more than 1,200 miles

19
00:02:27,601 --> 00:02:29,040
on their journey to the ocean,

20
00:02:29,041 --> 00:02:35,580
gaining volume and power as many streams merge to form one of the world's most violent rivers

21
00:02:35,581 --> 00:02:38,180
slicing apart the land and its path.

22
00:02:39,140 --> 00:02:43,560
In central Washington state the Columbia passes over high prehistoric bedrock

23
00:02:43,561 --> 00:02:48,880
near a large dry gorge carved out by the action of glaciers millions of years ago.

24
00:02:49,460 --> 00:02:52,040
This is the site of the Grand Coulee Dam.

25
00:02:52,940 --> 00:02:56,100
The design of this modern marvel had three goals.

26
00:02:56,101 --> 00:03:00,160
dam the flow of the river to regulate water passing downstream

27
00:03:00,161 --> 00:03:05,220
create a giant reservoir to power electrical generators inside the dam

28
00:03:05,221 --> 00:03:07,740
and raise the height of the Columbia River

29
00:03:07,741 --> 00:03:11,360
so that water could be siphoned into the Grand Coulee Gorge

30
00:03:11,361 --> 00:03:15,940
creating a lake to feed an irrigation system for the entire region.

31
00:03:17,080 --> 00:03:21,800
The tremendous scope of this endeavor demanded a concentration of human will

32
00:03:21,801 --> 00:03:23,480
unmatched in U.S. history

33
00:03:24,140 --> 00:03:27,320
But support for the dam was not universal.

34
00:03:28,160 --> 00:03:30,300
As America entered the Great Depression,

35
00:03:30,840 --> 00:03:35,260
politicians trying to stretch limited resources and private power companies

36
00:03:35,261 --> 00:03:38,240
who wanted to monopolize the sale of electricity

37
00:03:38,241 --> 00:03:40,880
would try to stop the Grand Coulee Project

38
00:03:40,881 --> 00:03:44,100
and place the future of Washington State at risk.

39
00:03:44,101 --> 00:03:58,101
America's Pacific Northwest has always been a volatile area prone to geological calamity.

40
00:03:59,340 --> 00:04:06,000
Forty million years ago, this land was a thick soup of molten rock, liquid granite nearly two miles deep.

41
00:04:06,840 --> 00:04:12,000
Then during the Ice Age, glaciers sliced the stone like the hard, cold crush of diamonds.

42
00:04:12,001 --> 00:04:17,200
leaving behind towering plateaus and the barren landscape.

43
00:04:18,400 --> 00:04:24,040
As this upheaval subsided, raging rivers formed from the melting snows of the Cascade Mountains

44
00:04:24,041 --> 00:04:26,740
carved paths through the arid plain.

45
00:04:28,040 --> 00:04:32,760
Though the land was somewhat forbidding, man has managed to thrive in this rugged territory.

46
00:04:33,360 --> 00:04:37,780
People like the Spokane tribe and the Nez Perce Nation lived there for centuries.

47
00:04:38,720 --> 00:04:43,480
These were the Indians which explorers Lewis and Clark stumbled upon in 1802,

48
00:04:44,080 --> 00:04:47,200
opening the territory to the so-called modern world.

49
00:04:48,120 --> 00:04:52,560
With the modern world came attempts to tame the land through agriculture and farming.

50
00:04:55,000 --> 00:04:59,140
The dust of ancient stone giants proved to be fertile soil,

51
00:04:59,620 --> 00:05:01,480
but it needed water to blossom.

52
00:05:02,420 --> 00:05:06,400
Unfortunately, the turbulent streams and rivers were uncontrollable.

53
00:05:07,260 --> 00:05:11,260
Sometimes there was too much water, most times not enough.

54
00:05:12,180 --> 00:05:18,400
The greatest tyrant of all, the Columbia, was like an irrational child, petulant and prone to tantrums,

55
00:05:19,240 --> 00:05:24,900
careening across the land with an astounding 110,000 cubic feet of water every second.

56
00:05:26,100 --> 00:05:30,500
At the turn of the century, farming was a tough life that was to get even tougher.

57
00:05:30,501 --> 00:05:36,460
A creeping continental drought in the 20s and 30s turned Washington State into a dust bowl.

58
00:05:37,640 --> 00:05:44,380
The area was enveloped by sandstorms so enormous they could be seen approaching for more than 17 miles.

59
00:05:45,760 --> 00:05:48,420
Thousands of farm animals died of thirst.

60
00:05:49,140 --> 00:05:52,340
Those which survived were little more than skin and bones.

61
00:05:54,020 --> 00:05:58,440
Farmers cursed the Columbia which refused to be tamed for irrigation.

62
00:05:58,441 --> 00:06:03,180
the sun turned their crops into a dry powder

63
00:06:03,181 --> 00:06:08,060
when the people of Washington State appealed to the federal government for help

64
00:06:08,061 --> 00:06:13,360
the Hoover administration claimed the territory had already received more than its share of public assistance

65
00:06:13,361 --> 00:06:16,980
but then it would have been hard for the president to hear anything

66
00:06:16,981 --> 00:06:21,200
over the resounding crash of the stock market in October 1929

67
00:06:21,201 --> 00:06:27,900
losses totaling more than 50 billion dollars sent the national economy into a tailspin

68
00:06:27,901 --> 00:06:41,901
By 1933, the American economy had hit rock bottom.

69
00:06:45,800 --> 00:06:47,760
The country was in chaos.

70
00:06:48,520 --> 00:06:53,520
On the heels of the carefree Roaring Twenties, 12 million men found themselves out of work.

71
00:06:53,521 --> 00:06:58,760
Their families torn apart as they looked for handouts selling whatever they could,

72
00:06:59,380 --> 00:07:01,640
taking any job no matter what the pay.

73
00:07:04,580 --> 00:07:08,880
Women and children stood for hours on food lines desperate for a meal,

74
00:07:09,320 --> 00:07:11,180
cup of soup, a crust of bread.

75
00:07:11,900 --> 00:07:15,540
Hundreds of thousands of teenagers became aimless drifters,

76
00:07:15,960 --> 00:07:19,300
begging and stealing to survive a harsh day-to-day existence.

77
00:07:19,301 --> 00:07:26,980
In 1932, thousands of World War I veterans marched on Washington

78
00:07:26,981 --> 00:07:29,840
demanding their bonus pay for combat service.

79
00:07:30,980 --> 00:07:36,980
They were promptly turned away, and federal troops set their cardboard shacks ablaze.

80
00:07:37,080 --> 00:07:41,100
Our group are loyal to the flag, loyal to the Constitution,

81
00:07:41,720 --> 00:07:44,260
loyal to the country, and loyal to the veterans.

82
00:07:44,261 --> 00:07:51,560
In cities across the country, the National Guard was put on alert in anticipation of violent civil unrest.

83
00:07:53,360 --> 00:07:55,360
Farm prices plummeted.

84
00:07:56,300 --> 00:08:00,040
Corn, which had been sold for 60 cents a bushel, was now 3 cents.

85
00:08:01,220 --> 00:08:04,040
Foreclosures swept across the land like a plague.

86
00:08:04,660 --> 00:08:09,940
At the height of the Depression, more than 1,000 families a day were evicted from their farms.

87
00:08:09,941 --> 00:08:14,880
chair croppers lined the highways with no place to go

88
00:08:14,881 --> 00:08:18,360
huddled in makeshift shelters called Hoovervilles

89
00:08:18,361 --> 00:08:21,020
a cynical reference to President Hoover

90
00:08:21,021 --> 00:08:24,040
who had promised the American people a chicken in every pot

91
00:08:24,041 --> 00:08:26,020
two cars in every garage

92
00:08:26,021 --> 00:08:28,860
families who had given their lives to the land

93
00:08:28,861 --> 00:08:31,560
were suspended in a world without hope

94
00:08:31,561 --> 00:08:34,240
my wife and I were forced out on the farm

95
00:08:34,241 --> 00:08:37,920
because we hadn't gotten no day labor to do

96
00:08:37,921 --> 00:08:42,880
We're only getting six bits in the dollar a day, and we can't make an honest, decent living at it.

97
00:08:43,500 --> 00:08:47,020
And that's the reason we're out here on Highway 61 right today.

98
00:08:48,460 --> 00:08:53,120
Back in the Northwest, hordes of migrant farmers descended on the territory,

99
00:08:53,840 --> 00:08:57,100
lured by the prospect of working on the Grand Coulee Dam.

100
00:08:57,640 --> 00:08:58,780
Many were turned away.

101
00:09:01,880 --> 00:09:05,980
It's a mighty hard road that our poor hand has pulled.

102
00:09:05,981 --> 00:09:13,140
And our poor feet has traveled a hot, dusty road

103
00:09:13,141 --> 00:09:21,380
Out of the dust bowl and westward we rode

104
00:09:21,381 --> 00:09:25,260
And your deserts are hot and your mountains are cold

105
00:09:25,261 --> 00:09:33,100
We travel with the wind and the rain in our face

106
00:09:33,101 --> 00:09:37,420
Our families migrating from place unto place

107
00:09:37,421 --> 00:09:43,460
We'll work in your beet fields till sundown tonight

108
00:09:43,461 --> 00:09:47,580
Travel 300 miles before the morning gets light

109
00:09:47,581 --> 00:09:56,360
Arizona, California, we'll make all your crops

110
00:09:56,361 --> 00:10:02,960
Then it's northward to Oregon to gather your hearts

111
00:10:02,961 --> 00:10:05,920
Strawberries, cherries and apples

112
00:10:05,921 --> 00:10:08,600
The best in that land full of promise

113
00:10:08,601 --> 00:10:10,180
At Pacific Northwest

114
00:10:10,181 --> 00:10:15,460
But conditions in that land full of promise

115
00:10:15,461 --> 00:10:17,720
Were no better than in the rest of the country

116
00:10:17,721 --> 00:10:20,480
Though the situation seemed bleak

117
00:10:20,481 --> 00:10:22,940
All the elements were in place

118
00:10:22,941 --> 00:10:25,580
From which a spectacular modern marvel

119
00:10:25,581 --> 00:10:28,340
Would emerge to raise the hopes of a nation

120
00:10:28,341 --> 00:10:31,600
And bring life back to a parched land

121
00:10:32,540 --> 00:10:35,700
There were the river, the men, and the need.

122
00:10:36,320 --> 00:10:38,200
All that was needed was a catalyst,

123
00:10:38,201 --> 00:10:41,700
a man who would declare war on the Depression

124
00:10:41,701 --> 00:10:44,780
and offer America a new deal.

125
00:10:45,560 --> 00:10:49,200
We now return to the Grand Coulee Dam on Modern Marvels.

126
00:10:50,420 --> 00:10:53,180
Let me assert my firm belief

127
00:10:53,181 --> 00:10:59,780
that the only thing we have to fear is fear itself.

128
00:10:59,781 --> 00:11:03,820
He came from a family of considerable means.

129
00:11:04,700 --> 00:11:08,080
He was a distant relation to the popular president, Teddy Roosevelt.

130
00:11:08,900 --> 00:11:14,780
Yet in 1921, when Franklin Delano Roosevelt was struck down by polio at the age of 29,

131
00:11:15,520 --> 00:11:18,040
it looked as though his political career was over.

132
00:11:18,720 --> 00:11:20,480
Doctors claimed he would never walk again.

133
00:11:20,481 --> 00:11:26,920
But he taught himself to crawl from room to room, then to stand on stiff metal braces.

134
00:11:26,921 --> 00:11:32,560
And by 1932, FDR was ready to help the country get back on its feet.

135
00:11:32,640 --> 00:11:37,980
It looks, my friends, like a real landslide this time.

136
00:11:40,400 --> 00:11:47,580
When Roosevelt's presidential campaign carried 46 of the 48 states, it was a national call to arms.

137
00:11:48,240 --> 00:11:54,120
FDR was change, the most significant change of all, a new attitude and a new hope.

138
00:11:54,121 --> 00:11:57,860
Keep that faith constant. Keep that faith high.

139
00:11:58,420 --> 00:12:02,060
Calling a special session of Congress, Roosevelt got right to work.

140
00:12:02,600 --> 00:12:07,140
Agricultural and industrial projects were introduced to prime the pomp of the economy.

141
00:12:07,980 --> 00:12:12,860
Hungry teenagers were given the opportunity to earn room and board in public work camps

142
00:12:12,861 --> 00:12:15,260
run by General Douglas MacArthur.

143
00:12:15,940 --> 00:12:21,260
And disheartened workers were given a fresh start in government-sponsored construction projects.

144
00:12:21,261 --> 00:12:21,961
projects.

145
00:12:21,962 --> 00:12:27,420
This was an era when America's infrastructure would be built to support the nation into

146
00:12:27,421 --> 00:12:33,060
the next century. Behind the banner of the National Recovery Act and its symbol of a

147
00:12:33,061 --> 00:12:40,652
Blue Eagle, the American people rallied. The Blue Eagle's loyal legions on parade.

148
00:12:40,653 --> 00:12:41,353
Soldier

149
00:12:41,354 --> 00:12:46,480
and sailor, millionaire and office boy, society matron and shop girl, elbow to elbow in the

150
00:12:46,481 --> 00:12:47,340
NRA Army.

151
00:12:51,260 --> 00:12:59,360
Crowds estimated that over 2.5 million people jammed sidewalks and overrun police lines to cheer the marches.

152
00:13:00,000 --> 00:13:02,580
It's a hard day's work for the cops, but they don't mind.

153
00:13:02,920 --> 00:13:04,080
It's in a great cause.

154
00:13:04,740 --> 00:13:07,200
For 10 hours, a stirring spectacle goes on.

155
00:13:07,740 --> 00:13:10,380
An amazing expression of American loyalty.

156
00:13:13,240 --> 00:13:19,240
Though some members of Congress wondered whether Roosevelt's massive water control project in the Tennessee Valley

157
00:13:19,241 --> 00:13:22,820
was guided by a capitalist philosophy or a socialist hand.

158
00:13:23,340 --> 00:13:26,560
Roosevelt insisted his efforts were neither fish nor fowl,

159
00:13:26,860 --> 00:13:29,500
but would taste mighty good to the American people.

160
00:13:29,840 --> 00:13:34,320
Steelworkers of the Smoky City thinks that the NRA is 100% fine.

161
00:13:35,180 --> 00:13:38,240
I think the NRA is a good thing.

162
00:13:38,960 --> 00:13:44,100
If we get six hours a day, one dollar per hour or more,

163
00:13:44,101 --> 00:13:49,420
I think this will put more men back to work again.

164
00:13:50,660 --> 00:13:53,320
Roosevelt's attention was soon drawn to the West.

165
00:13:53,840 --> 00:13:58,120
He saw that Washington state farmers desperately needed an irrigation system.

166
00:13:58,560 --> 00:14:04,540
But first he had to finish the Boulder Dam, a project initiated by the Hoover administration.

167
00:14:04,541 --> 00:14:05,540
Okay.

168
00:14:08,960 --> 00:14:12,540
Located on the Colorado River between Nevada and Arizona,

169
00:14:12,860 --> 00:14:18,380
the Boulder Dam, now officially known as the Hoover Dam, would take five years to build.

170
00:14:27,040 --> 00:14:31,120
The design of a dam is based on the layout of the site.

171
00:14:31,700 --> 00:14:35,920
Broad riverbeds with soft banks often require a gravity-type dam,

172
00:14:36,300 --> 00:14:41,420
in which the sheer weight of the structure keeps it in place against the force of the water behind it.

173
00:14:42,080 --> 00:14:45,100
In narrow canyon areas, an arch dam is more efficient,

174
00:14:45,680 --> 00:14:50,380
using its curved shape to transfer the weight of the water to the rock walls that support it.

175
00:14:51,220 --> 00:14:53,400
Boulder Dam is based on this concept.

176
00:14:53,401 --> 00:14:56,700
Although different in design from the Grand Coulee Dam,

177
00:14:57,220 --> 00:15:00,380
Boulder Dam shares many of the same construction challenges.

178
00:15:00,381 --> 00:15:05,140
For this reason, the Boulder Dam project was, in a sense, a testing ground

179
00:15:05,141 --> 00:15:08,940
for the technical problems to be faced at Grand Coulee.

180
00:15:11,200 --> 00:15:18,380
The people that came here that really built the dam were, many of them were from Boulder Dam.

181
00:15:19,460 --> 00:15:24,560
And so the experience they had gained there was very useful.

182
00:15:24,561 --> 00:15:31,740
Like the mighty Columbia, the Colorado River is also a great untamed giant.

183
00:15:32,220 --> 00:15:36,920
Prone to violent flooding, lives and land needed a dam to control the water.

184
00:15:42,360 --> 00:15:47,760
Among the techniques pioneered on the Boulder Dam project was the extensive use of explosives

185
00:15:47,761 --> 00:15:51,280
to remove millions of years of unstable rock sediment.

186
00:15:51,281 --> 00:16:01,740
High scaling workers and explosive experts called Powder Monkey spent two years setting off detonations in Black Canyon before the first concrete was laid.

187
00:16:05,340 --> 00:16:10,120
Then giant tunnels were blasted away to form diversionary channels for the river.

188
00:16:11,520 --> 00:16:17,880
This left the main riverbed dry so they could pour the enormous 660 foot concrete base of the dam.

189
00:16:17,881 --> 00:16:24,880
Two million cubic yards of sand were removed to expose bedroom.

190
00:16:24,881 --> 00:16:33,880
The concrete was blended from trainloads of cement of mountains of aggregate rock mined 12 miles north of the dam site.

191
00:16:33,881 --> 00:16:39,880
To keep concrete flowing to the forms, two giant mixing facilities were built.

192
00:16:39,881 --> 00:16:44,880
One upstream and one downstream of the dam.

193
00:16:44,881 --> 00:16:49,880
Delivering the final mix to the forms was an acrobatic feat.

194
00:16:49,881 --> 00:16:55,880
Nine aerial tramways would fly 16-ton loads right over the workers' heads.

195
00:16:55,881 --> 00:17:00,880
By remote control, operators would place the buckets at specific locations.

196
00:17:00,881 --> 00:17:05,880
The process involved hundreds of thousands of deliveries.

197
00:17:05,881 --> 00:17:10,880
Night and day. Every day. For years.

198
00:17:14,880 --> 00:17:27,260
The pipes used to direct the river through the giant turbines of the electrical generators

199
00:17:27,261 --> 00:17:31,480
were up to 30 feet in diameter, large enough to drive a train through.

200
00:17:33,860 --> 00:17:39,120
Steel plates from the east coast were shipped to a special fabrication plant at the dam site,

201
00:17:39,121 --> 00:17:41,100
where it was rolled into arcs.

202
00:17:41,101 --> 00:17:49,060
The plates were two and three-quarter inches thick and required 3,000 tons of pressure to produce the proper curvature.

203
00:17:51,160 --> 00:17:53,120
Three plates equaled a ring.

204
00:17:53,780 --> 00:17:55,260
Three rings equaled a section.

205
00:17:56,060 --> 00:17:59,020
Each section weighed 184 tons.

206
00:18:01,200 --> 00:18:06,620
Eventually, three miles of this bike were installed inside the solid rock of the canyon walls

207
00:18:06,621 --> 00:18:10,340
to channel water through the turbines of Boulder Dam's generators.

208
00:18:11,100 --> 00:18:14,360
All this work required thousands of workers.

209
00:18:20,940 --> 00:18:26,100
An entire town was built to accommodate their housing, transportation, and food.

210
00:18:27,720 --> 00:18:31,260
Commissaries designed to feed 1,500 men in a single sitting

211
00:18:31,261 --> 00:18:35,420
gave many of them something they hadn't enjoyed since the Depression began.

212
00:18:36,060 --> 00:18:36,920
A hot meal.

213
00:18:41,100 --> 00:18:48,080
A dedicated workforce helped bring Boulder Dam online two years ahead of schedule.

214
00:18:51,020 --> 00:18:55,300
Along with a sense of accomplishment, the workers gained valuable experience,

215
00:18:55,860 --> 00:19:00,580
know-how that would soon be used to build an even greater dam at the Grand Coulee.

216
00:19:01,820 --> 00:19:06,620
Boulder Dam had broken all the records, but it wasn't to wear the crown for long.

217
00:19:06,621 --> 00:19:13,820
The Grand Coulee Dam has enough concrete in it to lay a 16-foot highway

218
00:19:13,821 --> 00:19:18,220
from New York City to Seattle to Los Angeles and back to New York.

219
00:19:19,060 --> 00:19:21,240
Modern marvels will continue in a moment.

220
00:19:21,900 --> 00:19:24,900
In the name of the people of the United States,

221
00:19:25,960 --> 00:19:31,820
to whom you, Boulder Dam, are a symbol of greater things in the future,

222
00:19:31,821 --> 00:19:37,140
and in the honored presence of guests from many nations,

223
00:19:37,960 --> 00:19:40,460
I call you to life.

224
00:19:49,760 --> 00:19:52,980
When power started flowing from Boulder Dam,

225
00:19:53,140 --> 00:19:56,100
the celebration stretched more than 400 miles,

226
00:19:56,760 --> 00:19:58,360
all the way to Los Angeles,

227
00:19:58,361 --> 00:20:02,460
where the streets were the stage for an enormous electrical parade.

228
00:20:03,100 --> 00:20:09,100
But what was happening at Boulder Dam was beginning to worry the big private power companies in Washington state,

229
00:20:09,400 --> 00:20:13,300
which weren't interested in cheaper electrical alternatives for their customers.

230
00:20:13,560 --> 00:20:18,740
The opposition started, came from Spokane, where the Washington Power Company

231
00:20:18,741 --> 00:20:24,720
and other power companies in the country were charging out landish rates for the power.

232
00:20:24,721 --> 00:20:29,440
so they didn't want any public power with cheaper rates in here

233
00:20:29,441 --> 00:20:35,460
so they proposed a canal 135 miles long from Lake Pend Oreille

234
00:20:35,461 --> 00:20:38,440
to the Columbia Basin avoiding this dam.

235
00:20:39,480 --> 00:20:44,520
But the Roosevelt administration was determined to fight the stranglehold of the monopolies.

236
00:20:44,521 --> 00:20:49,040
I believe that the individual should have full liberty of action to make the most of himself

237
00:20:49,041 --> 00:20:53,080
but I do not believe that in the name of that sacred word

238
00:20:53,081 --> 00:20:58,720
a few powerful interests should be permitted to make industrial cannon fodder

239
00:20:58,721 --> 00:21:02,460
of the lives of half the population of the United States.

240
00:21:03,600 --> 00:21:06,320
Through emotional public service films,

241
00:21:06,880 --> 00:21:09,620
the government mounted a campaign to educate people

242
00:21:09,621 --> 00:21:12,400
about the benefits of owning their own power systems.

243
00:21:13,580 --> 00:21:16,320
It was the first time communities had been encouraged

244
00:21:16,321 --> 00:21:19,600
to resist the encroachment of private power companies.

245
00:21:23,080 --> 00:21:24,820
Distribute electricity at cost.

246
00:21:25,420 --> 00:21:29,720
The city voted 10 to 1 to buy the local company lines at a fair price

247
00:21:29,721 --> 00:21:32,460
and establish its own municipal system.

248
00:21:33,080 --> 00:21:36,600
For not a kilowatt of electricity will be retailed from these lines.

249
00:21:37,180 --> 00:21:40,960
That is the people's job, the responsibility of each community.

250
00:21:41,900 --> 00:21:45,340
It is up to the people to decide how they will get this power,

251
00:21:45,800 --> 00:21:49,340
through their publicly owned systems or through private power companies.

252
00:21:49,900 --> 00:21:51,620
The decision is theirs to make.

253
00:21:51,621 --> 00:21:55,380
The promise of power for every corner of the Northwest.

254
00:21:56,200 --> 00:21:59,280
Power to make a million and a quarter acres bloom again.

255
00:22:00,060 --> 00:22:02,920
Power to push the city to the farthest county line.

256
00:22:03,520 --> 00:22:07,420
To bring better crops and better living to the farmers of the region.

257
00:22:08,160 --> 00:22:09,140
Power for the home.

258
00:22:09,840 --> 00:22:11,440
Good life for Billy's eyes.

259
00:22:11,900 --> 00:22:13,320
Electric cooking for mother.

260
00:22:14,060 --> 00:22:16,660
The comfort of electric heat and electric coal.

261
00:22:17,280 --> 00:22:20,020
Leisure to replace the burdens of an outworn era.

262
00:22:20,780 --> 00:22:24,060
Power for millions of Americans who look westward hopefully.

263
00:22:24,540 --> 00:22:25,940
For land and jobs.

264
00:22:26,380 --> 00:22:28,100
For security and happiness.

265
00:22:28,880 --> 00:22:31,820
Power to make the American dream come true.

266
00:22:34,320 --> 00:22:41,180
In later years, when political winds changed to support the investment of big corporations in public utilities,

267
00:22:41,920 --> 00:22:44,100
these films were suppressed by the government.

268
00:22:44,740 --> 00:22:47,420
Complaints had been made that the films were anti-business

269
00:22:47,421 --> 00:22:50,180
and featured music performed by Woody Guthrie,

270
00:22:50,700 --> 00:22:54,780
the folk singer and activist who often championed the cause of union workers.

271
00:22:56,020 --> 00:22:58,380
But besides power and irrigation,

272
00:22:58,620 --> 00:23:01,000
there was an immediate problem with water control

273
00:23:01,001 --> 00:23:03,420
along the entire length of the Columbia River.

274
00:23:04,120 --> 00:23:08,140
Only a dam and gigantic reservoir could stop the continual flooding.

275
00:23:08,141 --> 00:23:13,980
with this in mind the years of bitter politics came to an end in 1933

276
00:23:13,981 --> 00:23:16,960
with the quick flourish of a presidential pen

277
00:23:16,961 --> 00:23:22,740
the American people and not big business would build and own the Grand Coulee Dam

278
00:23:32,480 --> 00:23:37,560
on September 9th 1933 work began on the Grand Coulee Dam

279
00:23:37,561 --> 00:23:39,240
land was surveyed.

280
00:23:39,900 --> 00:23:44,800
Ten communities were eliminated to make room for the backwash of the block Columbia River.

281
00:23:46,660 --> 00:23:51,200
700 graves of Native Americans were unearthed and relocated.

282
00:23:52,380 --> 00:23:54,200
And then the real construction started.

283
00:23:59,760 --> 00:24:05,620
The valley was rocked as explosives were used to remove millions of cubic yards of clay,

284
00:24:05,621 --> 00:24:12,620
sand and loose rock, deposited during the last ice age.

285
00:24:18,060 --> 00:24:22,500
Then the river was diverted to expose the bedrock which would support the massive weight of

286
00:24:22,501 --> 00:24:28,700
the structure. The world's largest coffer dams, temporary barriers that enclosed 60

287
00:24:28,701 --> 00:24:35,060
acre areas, allowed preparation of the river bed while water rushed past at nearly a million

288
00:24:35,061 --> 00:24:39,960
gallons a second, only inches from the workers. There was a lot of hand labor

289
00:24:39,961 --> 00:24:47,260
cleaning up the rock foundation, taking off the four or five feet of good rock,

290
00:24:47,900 --> 00:24:51,923
and it had to be as clean as the kitchen floor.

291
00:24:51,924 --> 00:24:55,860
Meanwhile, more than 10 million cubic yards of

292
00:24:55,861 --> 00:25:02,200
sand and gravel were excavated from a nearby pit, screened, washed, and piled on the east side of

293
00:25:02,201 --> 00:25:03,024
the river.

294
00:25:03,025 --> 00:25:09,200
By remote control, mixing plant operators determined the amount and size of

295
00:25:09,201 --> 00:25:15,240
the gravel carried by the conveyors, a rubber railroad more than a mile long which carried

296
00:25:15,241 --> 00:25:26,971
the gravel to stockpiles near the dam site at a rate of 40 carloads an hour.

297
00:25:26,972 --> 00:25:28,360
Cement by

298
00:25:28,361 --> 00:25:32,160
the train load was moved into silos by means of powerful pumps.

299
00:25:33,380 --> 00:25:36,340
Then the blended cement went through a 6,000-foot

300
00:25:36,341 --> 00:25:40,240
pipeline to the concrete mixing plant, where batches were

301
00:25:40,241 --> 00:25:44,500
weighed out on scales. Enormous compressed

302
00:25:44,501 --> 00:25:47,960
air and electrical controls operated the gates and measurements.

303
00:25:49,260 --> 00:25:52,360
Revolving chutes delivered a four-yard batch to one

304
00:25:52,361 --> 00:25:56,400
of four mixers, dumping and mixing timed automatically.

305
00:25:58,360 --> 00:26:10,360
Four-yard buckets, four to a flat car, were carried by diesel-electric locomotives

306
00:26:10,361 --> 00:26:15,700
to huge long-arm cranes on a high trestle spanning the dam site.

307
00:26:16,840 --> 00:26:22,240
Up to 42,000 tons of concrete were delivered in this fashion in a single day.

308
00:26:22,241 --> 00:26:29,580
Concrete was placed in forms 5 feet thick and 50 feet square

309
00:26:29,581 --> 00:26:33,760
Then vibrated mechanically to shake out air pockets

310
00:26:33,761 --> 00:26:40,460
As a final step, pipes were laid in the wet concrete

311
00:26:40,461 --> 00:26:44,660
Cold water would be run through the pipes to help cool the concrete

312
00:26:44,661 --> 00:26:48,000
Which otherwise would tend to heat and ferment as it set

313
00:26:52,240 --> 00:26:56,560
The hardened concrete was then sandblasted to create a consistent surface

314
00:26:56,561 --> 00:27:00,600
and then carefully hand-dried before the next block was poured.

315
00:27:02,100 --> 00:27:05,380
But not everything on the site ran according to plan.

316
00:27:05,700 --> 00:27:13,880
At one point in 1937, a sudden leak in a coffer dam started spilling 29,000 gallons of river water a minute.

317
00:27:14,500 --> 00:27:17,400
The workers used everything on hand to plug it,

318
00:27:17,401 --> 00:27:23,080
including mattresses, Christmas trees, wood shavings, and water-absorbing volcanic dust.

319
00:27:24,660 --> 00:27:32,720
Well, there's problems because the clay, the material here on the foundation over the rock

320
00:27:32,721 --> 00:27:41,480
is a highly underrated clay, very tough until it's disturbed.

321
00:27:41,481 --> 00:27:47,800
I took slides and you've seen some evidence of slides around the country here.

322
00:27:48,960 --> 00:27:53,740
When the ancient clay on one hillside threatened to slide into a construction area,

323
00:27:54,000 --> 00:27:57,680
it was frozen in place with refrigerated pipes for half a year

324
00:27:57,681 --> 00:28:00,260
until the workers could finish pouring cement.

325
00:28:01,420 --> 00:28:05,900
And in effect, they froze an arch dam of ice,

326
00:28:06,820 --> 00:28:11,160
about 12 feet wide, 40 feet vertical,

327
00:28:11,480 --> 00:28:13,060
and 120 feet long.

328
00:28:14,000 --> 00:28:18,060
It was dangerous work, which claimed the lives of 77 men,

329
00:28:18,480 --> 00:28:23,160
most from falling off the structure as it rose as high as a 50-story building.

330
00:28:23,960 --> 00:28:29,500
One construction company, Kaiser, opened a field hospital to care for injured workers,

331
00:28:29,501 --> 00:28:34,440
for these were largely inexperienced workers risking their lives to feed their families.

332
00:28:35,540 --> 00:28:39,040
Kaiser also instituted group medical coverage for its crews.

333
00:28:39,041 --> 00:28:43,520
This was the first time workers had ever been guaranteed coverage by CONTRAN.

334
00:28:44,000 --> 00:28:49,680
This innovative health care program became a model for other companies and governments around the world.

335
00:28:55,260 --> 00:29:02,020
The Grand Coulee Dam was the first man-made structure to exceed in volume the Great Pyramid of Keops.

336
00:29:02,900 --> 00:29:05,260
Modern marvels will continue in a moment.

337
00:29:05,261 --> 00:29:09,880
This was a make-work project.

338
00:29:10,200 --> 00:29:15,200
People came here from every place they could to get on the job.

339
00:29:15,520 --> 00:29:18,560
A lot of people in the state of Washington had migrated earlier

340
00:29:18,561 --> 00:29:21,120
to work in the orchards and in the fields.

341
00:29:21,121 --> 00:29:26,660
I think the original requirement was that residents would have preference on employment.

342
00:29:27,380 --> 00:29:30,600
Well, there weren't many dam builders here.

343
00:29:31,580 --> 00:29:32,340
None.

344
00:29:32,341 --> 00:29:43,020
A lot of men from off state came here and went to work, many of them under aliases.

345
00:29:44,620 --> 00:29:48,340
Using false IDs to slip past work residency requirements

346
00:29:48,940 --> 00:29:53,580
meant that many of the men who built the Grand Coulee Dam will forever remain anonymous.

347
00:29:54,340 --> 00:29:59,200
But during the construction, these rugged individuals created an equally rugged community,

348
00:29:59,201 --> 00:30:03,540
a company town run in the untamed tradition of the Wild West.

349
00:30:03,900 --> 00:30:08,180
Payday was Friday, and they also didn't have their wives with them here.

350
00:30:08,540 --> 00:30:12,720
Now, that doesn't mean they were morally corrupt, but that's the way it was.

351
00:30:12,920 --> 00:30:15,560
Everybody was out of a job. Everybody came here as broke.

352
00:30:16,420 --> 00:30:22,760
And so they went up the B Street, and that's what the honky-tonk part of town was.

353
00:30:23,360 --> 00:30:27,660
It was a western town, and there were a lot of beer parlors.

354
00:30:27,661 --> 00:30:28,880
above the beer parlors

355
00:30:28,881 --> 00:30:29,720
where the girls

356
00:30:29,721 --> 00:30:30,660
to entertain them.

357
00:30:31,500 --> 00:30:32,920
It was extremely hot

358
00:30:32,921 --> 00:30:34,600
in those flat roof places

359
00:30:34,601 --> 00:30:35,500
where the girls were

360
00:30:35,501 --> 00:30:36,860
above the beer parlors.

361
00:30:37,580 --> 00:30:38,740
And one of the customers

362
00:30:38,741 --> 00:30:39,660
used to go up there

363
00:30:39,661 --> 00:30:40,600
was Harvey Slocum

364
00:30:40,601 --> 00:30:42,080
who bid the job.

365
00:30:42,540 --> 00:30:43,240
He was told,

366
00:30:43,241 --> 00:30:44,980
well, the girl is so darn hot up here

367
00:30:44,981 --> 00:30:47,420
and he sent a plumbing crew up

368
00:30:47,421 --> 00:30:48,580
to put a sprinkling system

369
00:30:48,581 --> 00:30:49,420
on the whorehouse.

370
00:30:50,560 --> 00:30:52,760
The rooms above the beer parlors

371
00:30:52,761 --> 00:30:54,260
were not the only places

372
00:30:54,261 --> 00:30:55,820
that needed cooling off.

373
00:30:56,360 --> 00:31:01,800
Fires on B Street were a major problem because of the town's hastily constructed wooden buildings.

374
00:31:02,500 --> 00:31:06,100
Even rain brought no relief. The dust just became soup.

375
00:31:07,220 --> 00:31:10,920
Nobody who has ever worked at Grand Cooley Dan forgets the mud.

376
00:31:11,380 --> 00:31:12,420
We call it gumbo.

377
00:31:13,080 --> 00:31:18,820
The story of the day was put out by a power company man here.

378
00:31:18,821 --> 00:31:23,420
He said that he once saw a hat laying on the gumbo, and he picked it up,

379
00:31:23,421 --> 00:31:25,860
And up there was a fellow who looked right up at him.

380
00:31:26,140 --> 00:31:27,780
And the fellow said, well, don't be surprised.

381
00:31:27,860 --> 00:31:28,840
I'm sitting on a horse.

382
00:31:30,220 --> 00:31:32,460
Eventually, there were permanent communities

383
00:31:32,461 --> 00:31:34,740
where once there had only been mud.

384
00:31:35,200 --> 00:31:38,520
Of course, a lot of jobs going on besides building the dam.

385
00:31:38,521 --> 00:31:40,080
We had a town site to build.

386
00:31:40,660 --> 00:31:41,980
We had a bridge to build.

387
00:31:42,280 --> 00:31:44,480
A schoolhouse, highway down the hill,

388
00:31:44,620 --> 00:31:45,900
the railroad up the coulee.

389
00:31:46,080 --> 00:31:48,220
Those were just incidentals.

390
00:31:48,221 --> 00:31:53,520
They had a construction camp of tar paper shacks.

391
00:31:53,900 --> 00:31:57,620
We had a latrine, of course, but getting there,

392
00:31:57,720 --> 00:32:02,620
you had to go by another bunkhouse where the fellas took target practice out there.

393
00:32:03,320 --> 00:32:05,020
Back, through the back wall.

394
00:32:05,940 --> 00:32:10,420
Desperate for diversion, the men devised their own unique entertainments,

395
00:32:10,700 --> 00:32:16,140
like throwing a couple of desert tarantulas into a glass jar for an impromptu spider fight.

396
00:32:16,141 --> 00:32:20,380
but principally the men were there for one reason, work.

397
00:32:21,020 --> 00:32:22,520
A lot of them were inexperienced.

398
00:32:24,040 --> 00:32:25,680
They were willing to learn.

399
00:32:26,840 --> 00:32:31,240
The families underwent a lot of hardship at that time.

400
00:32:31,960 --> 00:32:35,920
Many lived under very primitive conditions in the environment here.

401
00:32:36,440 --> 00:32:37,660
They worked in crews.

402
00:32:38,540 --> 00:32:41,860
The crews were somewhat competitive as to progress

403
00:32:41,861 --> 00:32:46,560
and that went from the hand muckers to the shovel runners,

404
00:32:46,960 --> 00:32:49,080
the cat skinners, the truck drivers,

405
00:32:49,081 --> 00:32:53,760
everyone seemed to be motivated to get it done

406
00:32:53,761 --> 00:32:55,840
and it was noisy and it was dusty.

407
00:32:57,200 --> 00:33:02,060
In a very real sense, the Grand Coulee Dam was made of the strongest stuff on earth.

408
00:33:02,740 --> 00:33:05,320
12 million cubic yards of concrete

409
00:33:05,321 --> 00:33:09,360
reinforced by the fortitude of a largely nameless crew

410
00:33:09,361 --> 00:33:12,840
of more than 7,000 workers who risked their lives

411
00:33:12,841 --> 00:33:16,320
for an average salary of 85 cents an hour.

412
00:33:26,640 --> 00:33:30,820
It took eight years of grueling non-stop work.

413
00:33:31,620 --> 00:33:35,680
First to erect the enormous superstructure of the Grand Coulee Dam

414
00:33:35,681 --> 00:33:42,160
and then to install the first 24 massive turbines that would generate the electricity.

415
00:33:43,260 --> 00:33:46,860
Over the decade, six more generators would be added.

416
00:33:47,700 --> 00:33:52,940
While throughout the Washington farmland, more than 250 miles of canal were prepared

417
00:33:52,941 --> 00:33:56,880
to distribute irrigation water from the dam's mighty pumps.

418
00:33:58,720 --> 00:34:02,720
As the work continued, the dam became a popular tourist attraction,

419
00:34:02,721 --> 00:34:05,000
drawing 1,000 visitors a day.

420
00:34:06,480 --> 00:34:10,560
Grandstands were built to give the public a safe but spectacular view.

421
00:34:12,140 --> 00:34:15,840
VIPs could get an even closer look by riding on top of the structure.

422
00:34:17,640 --> 00:34:22,360
Some newspaper men like Hugh Blanc actually went inside the project.

423
00:34:23,260 --> 00:34:28,320
They needed to do some work inside of the tunnel because they needed to spread cable through it.

424
00:34:28,920 --> 00:34:33,920
But there was a leak in the concrete form so it filled part of the cable way up.

425
00:34:34,500 --> 00:34:39,320
So in order to get working men in there, slide them in there on a gliding platform,

426
00:34:39,640 --> 00:34:40,800
they had to put a cable through.

427
00:34:41,640 --> 00:34:45,080
This was the huge Grand Coulee Dam project in the state of Washington.

428
00:34:45,840 --> 00:34:49,440
And Evelyn's specialty was crawling through dirt-clogged drainage pipes

429
00:34:49,441 --> 00:34:51,180
with a string tied around her neck.

430
00:34:51,760 --> 00:34:54,820
The string served as a lead line tied to a steel cable

431
00:34:54,821 --> 00:34:57,460
strong enough to pull a workman through on a sled,

432
00:34:57,960 --> 00:34:59,980
cleaning the offending dirt out ahead of him.

433
00:34:59,981 --> 00:35:10,020
We had put a man at the far end, and then he'd get behind, hold it until the camera crew could get in the other side.

434
00:35:10,620 --> 00:35:15,360
And then with so much lights, so many lights, why, the cat wasn't going to come out.

435
00:35:15,460 --> 00:35:17,660
It was too noisy, too flashy.

436
00:35:18,300 --> 00:35:20,460
So I kept saying so-and-so.

437
00:35:20,560 --> 00:35:25,140
Finally I said, Charlie, my friend, bark like a dog, bark like a dog.

438
00:35:25,280 --> 00:35:26,820
I said, damn it, bark like a dog.

439
00:35:26,860 --> 00:35:28,300
And he did, and it came right out.

440
00:35:29,160 --> 00:35:31,900
Some might say a small dog could do the job.

441
00:35:32,280 --> 00:35:35,160
Well, that's true, except that dogs are always the heroes.

442
00:35:35,640 --> 00:35:37,100
Cats seldom get the opportunity.

443
00:35:37,740 --> 00:35:41,600
And we think it highly commendable that dog's best friend gave a cat

444
00:35:41,601 --> 00:35:43,700
a chance to lead him out of the darkness

445
00:35:43,701 --> 00:35:48,080
on what was then one of the biggest dam projects in the world.

446
00:35:50,620 --> 00:35:53,980
Despite early claims that the project was just a white elephant,

447
00:35:53,981 --> 00:35:58,060
the public gradually came to view the dam as an object of beauty

448
00:35:59,020 --> 00:36:02,820
In 1941, as the Mammoth facility prepared to go online,

449
00:36:03,340 --> 00:36:05,840
plans were made for a grand opening ceremony.

450
00:36:07,160 --> 00:36:12,620
One gallon of water for each of 48 states was poured over the dam in the spirit of national unity.

451
00:36:13,400 --> 00:36:16,520
Arizona, still fuming over a water rights dispute,

452
00:36:16,521 --> 00:36:21,300
only sent a half a gallon, claiming California had stolen the other half.

453
00:36:22,100 --> 00:36:25,020
Native Americans blessed the dam's opening day.

454
00:36:25,021 --> 00:36:31,680
while inside workers and engineers anxiously watched the first spin of the mighty turbines.

455
00:36:32,360 --> 00:36:37,720
All the work, all the money, all the promises and dreams of the future converged at this moment.

456
00:36:38,620 --> 00:36:41,380
When Washington's governor, Arthur Langley, gave the signal,

457
00:36:41,700 --> 00:36:43,980
the flick of the switch started the pumping operation.

458
00:36:43,981 --> 00:36:48,360
Suddenly more than 21,000 cubic feet of water per second

459
00:36:48,361 --> 00:36:53,700
came pouring through the pipes into the canal that feeds the Grand Coulee.

460
00:36:55,020 --> 00:36:59,440
The resulting man-made lake changed the shape of the territory forever.

461
00:37:00,400 --> 00:37:05,260
Likewise, six and a half million kilowatts was soon coursing through cables,

462
00:37:05,261 --> 00:37:10,220
and this new source of power would give rise to new industries.

463
00:37:11,020 --> 00:37:18,060
Aluminum manufacturing, aircraft construction, later nuclear research.

464
00:37:19,500 --> 00:37:24,640
So the power of the Columbia truly became America's strong right arm.

465
00:37:25,020 --> 00:37:30,100
Light lines of liberty taking a million horsepower out of the river canyon.

466
00:37:31,040 --> 00:37:33,820
2,500 miles of shining circuits.

467
00:37:35,660 --> 00:37:40,400
Here at Grand Coulee Dam was high technology with a rural touch.

468
00:37:41,200 --> 00:37:45,080
As ranchers led their herds across the mile-long stretch,

469
00:37:45,600 --> 00:37:48,260
the images seemed to mark an American transition,

470
00:37:48,980 --> 00:37:56,040
A yellow brick road journey to a place where the magic of nature had been harnessed to do the bidding of man.

471
00:37:56,860 --> 00:37:59,740
Behind me and down in the valley you see Grand Coulee Dam.

472
00:38:00,420 --> 00:38:05,000
It's a multi-purpose project that was built by the Bureau of Reclamation here on the Columbia River.

473
00:38:05,340 --> 00:38:07,880
As you can see, it's a concrete gravity dam,

474
00:38:08,420 --> 00:38:14,720
which means that it's strictly the sheer weight of the concrete that holds it in place against the force of the Columbia River.

475
00:38:15,580 --> 00:38:19,380
The dam is about, just about a mile long from one end to the other,

476
00:38:19,800 --> 00:38:23,460
and it's 500 feet high, 500 feet thick at the base.

477
00:38:23,920 --> 00:38:29,260
If you took a cross-section of the dam, it would actually look sort of like a right triangle, like this.

478
00:38:29,900 --> 00:38:31,940
500 feet high, 500 feet thick.

479
00:38:32,380 --> 00:38:34,620
What you see sticking out of the water is about 300 feet.

480
00:38:35,040 --> 00:38:36,820
There's another 250 feet.

481
00:38:37,000 --> 00:38:39,660
It's actually underneath the water that goes down to the bedrock.

482
00:38:39,661 --> 00:38:47,000
This happens to be an area in eastern Washington where the granite bedrock is very close to the surface.

483
00:38:47,580 --> 00:38:51,320
It was very easy to get at, provided a very firm foundation for the dam.

484
00:38:52,260 --> 00:38:55,880
And there are almost 12 million cubic yards of concrete in the dam.

485
00:38:56,600 --> 00:38:59,560
So enough concrete to put a sidewalk the whole way around the equator.

486
00:39:01,500 --> 00:39:05,500
The lake behind the dam is about 150 miles long.

487
00:39:05,501 --> 00:39:06,689
It's called Franklin D.

488
00:39:06,690 --> 00:39:10,360
Roosevelt Lake or Lake Roosevelt, and it's actually the Columbia River.

489
00:39:11,120 --> 00:39:14,400
It contains about 9 million acre-feet of water.

490
00:39:15,600 --> 00:39:19,680
I'm standing at what we refer to as the headworks here at Grand Coulee.

491
00:39:20,180 --> 00:39:22,460
This is actually the beginning of the irrigation system.

492
00:39:23,060 --> 00:39:27,080
We take water from the Columbia River down behind the dam.

493
00:39:27,540 --> 00:39:29,320
There are huge pumps down under the water.

494
00:39:29,920 --> 00:39:34,480
I pick the water up, pump them up through these discharge tubes into a canal.

495
00:39:34,481 --> 00:39:40,540
Those pumps down there are huge pumps. They're as large as 75,000 horsepower each.

496
00:39:41,040 --> 00:39:47,540
One of those pumps running continuously would be enough to supply the city of Chicago with all of its water needs.

497
00:39:47,541 --> 00:40:01,541
The canal comes from the dam, carries water from the pumps for about a mile and a half,

498
00:40:05,220 --> 00:40:08,680
and then right down here, it empties out into Banks Lake.

499
00:40:09,280 --> 00:40:13,220
Banks Lake is the irrigation holding reservoir for the Columbia Basin project,

500
00:40:13,700 --> 00:40:15,580
and it sits inside the Grand Coulee.

501
00:40:15,581 --> 00:40:24,801
The word coulee is actually French and it's a geological term that means a canyon that's been carved out by glacial and water action that is dry.

502
00:40:24,802 --> 00:40:29,380
It does not have water flowing through it. And that's what we have here.

503
00:40:29,381 --> 00:40:34,340
We have this huge canyon that is sitting high and dry 300 feet above the lake.

504
00:40:34,341 --> 00:40:40,200
So it runs from this canyon wall here to the far canyon wall over here.

505
00:40:40,360 --> 00:40:41,820
It's about 60 miles long.

506
00:40:42,460 --> 00:40:44,920
What we've done is just going into the Grand Coulee

507
00:40:44,921 --> 00:40:48,360
and put in two small earthen dams about 30 miles apart,

508
00:40:49,060 --> 00:40:54,280
and we use that to hold the irrigation water that we pump up from the Columbia River.

509
00:40:57,620 --> 00:41:02,640
Today, more than half a million acres of farmland, bigger than several small states,

510
00:41:02,641 --> 00:41:05,920
are irrigated by the Columbia Basin Project.

511
00:41:06,960 --> 00:41:11,740
The dam generates more than 19 billion kilowatt hours of electricity,

512
00:41:12,260 --> 00:41:15,520
supplying communities as far south as the Mexican border.

513
00:41:15,940 --> 00:41:19,380
But the dam itself is more than a chunk of concrete.

514
00:41:20,540 --> 00:41:23,380
Grand Coulee isn't a solid chunk of concrete,

515
00:41:24,280 --> 00:41:27,040
even though it's almost 12 million cubic yards.

516
00:41:27,580 --> 00:41:30,880
There are corridors like this, about 14 miles of corridors

517
00:41:30,881 --> 00:41:32,200
that go back and forth through the dam.

518
00:41:32,640 --> 00:41:35,600
Some of them are inspection tunnels, some of them actually have equipment in it,

519
00:41:35,880 --> 00:41:38,360
valves and pumps and other pieces of equipment that we use.

520
00:41:38,940 --> 00:41:40,420
But they're not all finished like this.

521
00:41:42,080 --> 00:41:45,800
There is a mysterious world inside the dam,

522
00:41:45,801 --> 00:41:50,920
an eerie, lonely labyrinth that leads through the heart of the giant.

523
00:41:50,921 --> 00:42:04,921
We have a single line diagram of all our power systems here, our main power systems.

524
00:42:05,340 --> 00:42:13,700
We have a 115,000 volt switch yard, a 230,000 volt switch yard, and a half a million volt switch yard.

525
00:42:13,701 --> 00:42:19,720
All these switch yards do is distribute the power, take the power in from the generators,

526
00:42:20,360 --> 00:42:24,800
run them across a bus where all the power goes together, and distribute it to different lines.

527
00:42:25,600 --> 00:42:31,180
In the master control room, the engineers operate the flow of power with keyboard strokes.

528
00:42:31,800 --> 00:42:34,680
We keep track of the river flows continually.

529
00:42:35,500 --> 00:42:38,300
Everything has to be watched and corrected, adjusted.

530
00:42:38,301 --> 00:42:47,440
we regulate, we start-stop generators, just everything virtually can be done from here.

531
00:42:47,520 --> 00:42:50,140
Now we still have operators out there on stations doing jobs.

532
00:42:50,500 --> 00:42:54,580
We do need operators because the computer system doesn't work that well alone.

533
00:42:54,660 --> 00:42:56,180
It has to have people working with it.

534
00:42:56,920 --> 00:43:01,180
Since 1980, the dam has been primarily operated by computers,

535
00:43:01,580 --> 00:43:05,040
which constantly check and recheck the structure's integrity.

536
00:43:05,720 --> 00:43:10,180
That portion of the dam which is underwater is checked by a special maintenance submarine,

537
00:43:10,680 --> 00:43:14,020
which sends video back to the engineers on the surface.

538
00:43:23,100 --> 00:43:24,760
Coming down on it, coming down on it.

539
00:43:24,880 --> 00:43:25,900
Oh, the line's still there?

540
00:43:26,120 --> 00:43:27,140
Yes, the line's still there.

541
00:43:27,160 --> 00:43:27,880
On the last one.

542
00:43:29,180 --> 00:43:29,880
Here.

543
00:43:30,060 --> 00:43:31,940
I don't know if I can quite touch this one at the bottom.

544
00:43:31,941 --> 00:43:45,941
Meanwhile, on the water itself, sports enthusiasts put all 150 miles of Lake Roosevelt to good use.

545
00:43:48,860 --> 00:43:52,020
Each year the lake attracts vacationers from across the country.

546
00:43:52,680 --> 00:43:55,940
Today children can play where their parents once struggled.

547
00:43:57,140 --> 00:44:01,320
Here at the Grand Coulee Dam, there is still awe in the achievement.

548
00:44:01,940 --> 00:44:07,200
the rushing water still roars a reminder of the heroic and determined men

549
00:44:07,201 --> 00:44:11,560
who dreamed of brighter days they would never live to see.

550
00:44:12,480 --> 00:44:17,080
Look down in the canyon and there you will see

551
00:44:17,081 --> 00:44:23,620
Grand Cooley showers her blessings on me

552
00:44:23,621 --> 00:44:31,540
My land I'll defend with my life if it be

553
00:44:31,541 --> 00:44:36,380
Because my pastures are plenty, must always be free.

